course: dictionary
lesson: 5533
title: Leeward and the rain shadow
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wind blows from one direction. The side of something that faces the wind is called windward, and the side turned away from it is leeward. Leeward means the sheltered side, away from the wind.
explain: Read «The side of a mountain range facing away from the wet wind is often dry, which is called a rain shadow (that side is called the leeward side).» The clue is «away from the wet wind». The leeward side is sheltered from the wet wind, so it is often dry.
explain: Sailors use the word too. To leeward means in the direction the wind is blowing towards, so a boat drifting to leeward is pushed the way the wind goes. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: does «the sheltered side, away from the wind» still fit? If it does, you have it.
fact: Leeward means on the side away from the wind, the sheltered side.
fact: To leeward means in the direction the wind is blowing towards.
fact: Leeward is the opposite of windward, which is the side that faces the wind.
fact: In geography, the leeward side of a mountain is often dry. This is called a rain shadow.
fact: The Atacama Desert in Chile lies partly in the rain shadow of the Andes.
exercise: What does leeward mean? | * The sheltered side, away from the wind | The side that faces the wind | A kind of wet weather | The top of a hill
exercise: «...that side is called the leeward side.» Which word names the side away from the wind? | * leeward | mountain | rain | windward
exercise: Sailors say a boat drifts to leeward. Which way is the boat moving? | Against the wind | Round in a circle | Straight up into the sky | * In the direction the wind is blowing towards
test: Why is the Atacama Desert so dry? Part of it lies in the rain shadow of the Andes, which means it is on the ... side of the mountains. | * leeward | frozen | windward | sunny
test: Which word means the opposite of windward? | steep | * leeward | cold | wet
exam: Which sentence uses leeward correctly? | * The leeward side of the hill was sheltered from the wind. | The boat sailed leeward to the bottom of the sea. | The leeward hill was the wettest because it faced the wind. | Leeward is a loud noise made by the sea.
